nvidia is back with a new Android shield  now based on its powerful Tegra x1 chip  this slim 200 our box runs on the  android TV operating system offering the  usual content like controller optimized  android games and media apps but nvidia  has other tricks up its sleeve gaming is  central to the shield so Nvidia is going  the extra mile to deliver content that  can't be found on other android boxes  Nvidia secret sauce is the Tegra x1 and  it's working with publishers to deliver  console and PC grade content beyond  native android gaming you can stream  games from your PC like past shield  devices but the most important gaming  feature is shields ability to stream PC  games from in videos GTX 980 glazed  servers offering high end pc gaming on a  two-hundred-dollar box at least visually  that outpaces playstation 4 and xbox one  nvidia is also incentivizing people by  upgrading its grid service in the past  it would stream video at 720p and 30  frames per second but now grid can  support 1080p and 60 frames per second  while streaming to the new shield and  other shield devices I tested the  service on a consumer-grade comcast  internet connection and it was hands  down the best example of cloud game  streaming I've ever tried because the  games are running on a high-end GPU they  look amazing with minimal video  compression artifacts getting in the way  I played a combination of games  including grid 2 dying light ultra  street fighter 4 and metal gear solid 5  ground zeroes and Walter we're  perceptible latency issues on occasion  it only bothered me was street fighter 4  everything felt close to if not the same  as playing on a console I'll be it with  much better graphics new media's new  shield will cost around two hundred  dollars when it comes out hopefully  before summer a subscription model will  give you access to about 200 games as  well which is supposed to be priced  competitively with PlayStation Plus on  xbox live while you will have to pay  full price for new games that come to  grid nvidia is in negotiations with  valve to offer steam versions of games  with every grid purchase Nvidia may not  call it a console but let's be frank the  shield is a box that connects to your TV  plays the latest games for big  publishers and comes with a wireless  controller that sounds like a console to  me plus at two hundred dollars it may  give Sony and Microsoft a run for their  money if grid works is good in the real  world as it did during my demo time will  tell but one thing's for sure the future  of console gaming just got a lot more  interesting
 

